The Write tool is not available in this session, so I'll deliver the full plan directly as my response.

---

# Implementation Plan: Multiplayer Latency Optimization (`improve/mp-latency`)

Target: at 700–1100 ms RTT, block edits feel instant, remote entities stay smooth, and the wire gets ~70–85% cheaper — all wire-compatible with PROTOCOL_VERSION 2 (no bump anywhere in this plan).

## Verified ground truth the design rests on

- Replica never dispatches gameplay commands locally except `selectSlot` (`lib/net/NetworkSession.ts:216-223`); `placeBlock`/`attack` go up only, so today place/break/swing feedback arrives ~RTT later.
- `blockPlaced`/`blockBroken`/`attackSwung` events are **not attributed** — `GameEngine.emit` (`GameEngine.ts:1188`) stamps `playerId` only on `advancementUnlocked`. `TickMessage.ev` is already typed `Array<GameEvent & { playerId?: string }>` (`protocol.ts:174`), so stamping is wire-compatible.
- `BlockChangeTracker.set()` (`lib/game/engine/blockChanges.ts:31-45`) is the single chokepoint that relights (`applyEdit`) — a revert through it restores lighting for free. Its `editJournal` is `Map<idx, newBlock>` (no prev value) and is **never drained on the replica**.
- `tickMining(..., {cosmetic: true})` caps at `hardness * 0.99` (`mining.ts:140-147`); chests spill inventory on break (`spillChestOnBreak`), drops/XP/durability all mutate inventory.
- Fixed `INTERPOLATION_DELAY_MS = 125` (`interpolation.ts:7`); render time = `clock.estimatedServerTimeMs(now) - 125` (`NetworkSession.ts:628`); clock is pure EWMA α=0.2 on RTT **and offset** (`clock.ts:20-38`) — offset shifts on every jittery pong, which itself causes render-time pops. Ping every 2000 ms (`NetworkSession.ts:623`).
- Bun 1.3.12 `Bun.serve` websocket supports `perMessageDeflate?: boolean | { compress?: WebSocketCompressor | boolean; decompress?: ... }`, default **disabled** (`node_modules/bun-types/serve.d.ts:455-487`); compressor values `"disable" | "shared" | "dedicated" | "3KB" ... "256KB"`. `server/index.ts:81-116` sets no websocket options today.
- Poses serialize raw doubles (e.g. `256.04500000000002` ≈ 17 chars) via `JSON.stringify` (`codec.ts:11-17`).
- Sim latency is a constant symmetric `setTimeout` on send (`NetworkSession.ts:145-159`) and receive (`402-408`), seeded from `NEXT_PUBLIC_NET_SIM_LATENCY_MS` (`118-119`).
- Debug HUD: `DebugOverlay.tsx` (props from `MinecraftGame.tsx:147`; `online` session available at `MinecraftGame.tsx:158`). `ConnectionStatus.tsx` polls `session.rttMs()` at 1 Hz.
- Tests: `lib/net/NetworkSession.test.ts` (FakeSocket harness, 12 tests incl. "the simulated-latency knob defers sends" and "routes gameplay commands to the wire but keeps presentation on the replica"), `lib/net/protocol.test.ts` (**clock sync tests live here**, lines 98–113), `lib/net/interpolation.test.ts`, `server/room.test.ts` (~20 tests), `e2e/multiplayer.e2e.ts` (one test, two browser contexts).

---

## Slice 1 — Transport diet: pose quantization + permessage-deflate

Cheapest win, zero behavioral risk, lands first so later slices are measured on the new baseline.

**Files:** `lib/net/codec.ts`, `server/room.ts`, `server/index.ts`, `lib/net/NetworkSession.ts`, `lib/net/protocol.test.ts` (or new `codec.test.ts`), `server/room.test.ts`, docs.

**Quantization helpers — in `codec.ts`** (encoding concern, imported by both ends; protocol.ts stays shapes-only):

```ts
/** 2-decimal position quantizer (1 cm) — wire-size only, no shape change. */
export const qPos = (v: number): number => Math.round(v * 100) / 100;
/** 3-decimal angle/velocity quantizer (~0.06°). */
export const qAng = (v: number): number => Math.round(v * 1000) / 1000;
```

Apply at serialization sites only (shadows/deadbands keep full precision):
- `server/room.ts`: `collectPlayerPoses` (x/y/z→qPos, yaw/pitch→qAng), `collectMobPoses`, `collectVehiclePoses`, `collectProjectilePoses` (velocity via qAng is fine at 3dp — orientation-only on the client), `rosterEntry`, and the mounted `x/y/z` in `buildSelfDelta`.
- `lib/net/NetworkSession.ts`: the 20 Hz pose sender in `afterFrame` and the `sendCmd` eye pose.

No protocol bump: identical field names/types, just shorter number literals. Sanity margins: 0.005 blocks ≪ the `applyRemotePose` clamps (+0.5 slack) and ≪ `MOB_DEADBAND_SQ`.

**permessage-deflate — `server/index.ts`**, inside the `websocket: {...}` config:

```ts
websocket: {
  // JSON tick frames are ~1–4 KB of highly repetitive keys; deflate gets 3–5×.
  perMessageDeflate: { compress: "16KB", decompress: true },
  open(ws) { ... },
```

Browsers negotiate `permessage-deflate` automatically when offered — no client change. Bun's own WebSocket client (used by `scripts/loadSim.ts` / `netProbe.ts`) offers it by default too (`bun-types/bun.d.ts:4264-4277`), so loadSim exercises the compression path realistically.

Risk assessment (shared-cpu-2x, 8 players × 3 rooms = 24 sockets @20 Hz): ~480 deflates/s of ~1–2 KB frames with 16 KB dedicated windows ≈ tens of µs each — well under 5% of one core; window memory ≈ 24 × ~50 KB ≈ 1.2 MB, trivial against the 512 MB VM. Verify with loadSim `slowestTickMs` before/after (see Slice 7). Fallback knob if CPU shows up: `{ compress: "8KB", decompress: false }`.

**Measurement caveat to document:** `Room.bytesOut` counts **pre-compression** `encoded.length`, so `/rooms` `kbOutPerSec` shows the quantization win but not the deflate win. Deflate savings are verified in Chrome DevTools (WS frame sizes + `Sec-WebSocket-Extensions` response header) and Fly egress metrics — say so in tuning.md.

**Tests:**
- `codec`: `qPos/qAng` round-trip through `JSON.stringify` at ≤6/7 chars for typical coords; negative/zero/±π cases.
- `room.test.ts`: new assertion in an existing pose-flow test — broadcast `pp`/`mp` values have ≤2/3 decimals (e.g. `Number.isInteger(pose.x * 100)`). Check existing tests that assert exact float positions (e.g. vehicle/arrow test around line 180) — update expectations to quantized values.
- `server.e2e-smoke.test.ts`: confirm handshake still passes with deflate enabled (Bun client negotiates it).

**Docs:** `docs/protocol.md` (Transport & framing: note quantization precision and that permessage-deflate is negotiated, envelope unchanged), `docs/tuning.md` (Multiplayer networking: new bullet for `perMessageDeflate` + the bytesOut measurement caveat), `CHANGELOG.md` (Changed).

**Commit:** `net: quantize replicated poses and enable permessage-deflate`

---

## Slice 2 — Latency-sim jitter + client net observability (tooling before the features it verifies)

**Files:** `lib/net/NetworkSession.ts`, `components/game/DebugOverlay.tsx`, `components/MinecraftGame.tsx`, `lib/net/NetworkSession.test.ts`, docs.

**Sim upgrade — `NetworkSession`:**

```ts
setSimulatedLatency(ms: number, jitterMs?: number): void;   // extends existing signature
simulatedLatency(): number;                                  // unchanged (base ms)
simulatedJitter(): number;                                   // new getter
```

Per-message delay = `Math.max(0, ms + (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * jitterMs)` — `Math.random` is fine here (frame-local net tooling, never worldgen; note this in a comment). **Critical edge case: preserve FIFO.** TCP never reorders, and `applyBlocks`/seq logic assume ordering. Each direction keeps a `lastScheduledDeliveryMs`; the effective fire time is `max(now + delay, lastScheduledDeliveryMs)` (monotonic queue), applied in both `delayedSend` and `onServerFrame`. Env seeding: parse `NEXT_PUBLIC_NET_SIM_JITTER_MS` exactly like `NEXT_PUBLIC_NET_SIM_LATENCY_MS` at `NetworkSession.ts:118-119`.

**Client byte counters + stats surface:**

```ts
export type NetStats = {
  rttMs: number;            // EWMA (display)
  jitterMs: number;         // tick inter-arrival p90 deviation (Slice 3 fills this; 0 until then)
  interpDelayMs: number;    // effective interpolation delay (125 until Slice 3)
  inKBps: number;           // 2 s rolling window, counted in delayedSend / processServerFrame
  outKBps: number;
  pendingPredictions: number; // 0 until Slice 5
};
netStats(): NetStats;       // added to the NetworkSession type
```

Count `data.length` for text frames and `byteLength` for the binary world-sync (post-decompression approximation — note in the doc comment).

**F3 overlay:** `DebugOverlay` gains an optional prop `net?: NetworkSession | null` (pass `online` from `MinecraftGame.tsx:147`); inside, a 500 ms `setInterval` polls `net.netStats()` and appends two lines: `Net: 812 ms rtt, 96 ms jitter, interp 310 ms` and `Bandwidth: in 14.2 KB/s, out 2.1 KB/s, pred 2`. `ConnectionStatus` stays as-is (ping badge only).

**Tests (`NetworkSession.test.ts`, FakeSocket harness):**
- jitter never reorders: send 20 messages with `setSimulatedLatency(50, 200)`, assert socket receives them in seq order (existing "simulated-latency knob defers sends" test extends).
- `netStats()` counts in/out bytes across a scripted tick exchange.

**Docs:** `docs/tuning.md` (Latency sim bullet gains the jitter arg + env var), `docs/architecture.md` (debug handle mention: `setSimulatedLatency(ms, jitterMs)`), `CHANGELOG.md`.

**Commit:** `net: jittered latency simulation and F3 net stats`

---

## Slice 3 — Clock hardening + adaptive interpolation delay

**Files:** `lib/net/clock.ts`, `lib/net/interpolation.ts`, `lib/net/NetworkSession.ts`, new `lib/net/clock.test.ts` (move the "clock sync" describe out of `protocol.test.ts`), `lib/net/interpolation.test.ts`, docs.

**Clock hardening — `clock.ts`.** Keep the `ClockSync` surface; change internals to NTP-style best-sample offset with slewed application:

```ts
export function createClockSync(alpha = 0.2): ClockSync;
// internals:
//  - rtt: EWMA α=0.2 (unchanged — display + prediction timeouts)
//  - window: ring of last OFFSET_WINDOW_SIZE {rtt, offset} pong samples
//  - targetOffset = offset of the min-RTT sample in the window
//    (least queue-delay asymmetry ⇒ most trustworthy half-RTT split)
//  - appliedOffset slews toward targetOffset at OFFSET_SLEW_MS_PER_SEC inside
//    estimatedServerTimeMs (using nowMs deltas), except |error| > OFFSET_SNAP_MS → snap
//    (first sample and post-reconnect discontinuities snap too)
```

Constants: `OFFSET_WINDOW_SIZE = 16`, `OFFSET_SLEW_MS_PER_SEC = 40`, `OFFSET_SNAP_MS = 250`.

**Ping cadence:** extract `PING_INTERVAL_MS = 1000` in `NetworkSession.ts` (was inline `2000` at line 623). At 1 Hz the 16-sample window spans 16 s; a ping frame is ~40 bytes — negligible.

**Adaptive delay — `interpolation.ts`** (stays pure math, DOM-free):

```ts
export const INTERPOLATION_DELAY_MIN_MS = 125;   // old fixed value becomes the floor
export const INTERPOLATION_DELAY_MAX_MS = 450;
export const INTERPOLATION_JITTER_MULT = 2;
export const DELAY_SLEW_MS_PER_SEC = 60;         // ≤6% render-time warp, imperceptible
const JITTER_WINDOW = 64;                        // ~3.2 s of tick arrivals

export type DelayController = {
  /** Feed one tick frame's arrival (client wall clock + server tick number). */
  onTickArrival(nowMs: number, tickN: number): void;
  /** Slewed effective delay for this frame (call once per rAF). */
  effectiveDelayMs(nowMs: number): number;
  /** p90 |inter-arrival − nominal| over the window (for netStats). */
  jitterMs(): number;
};
export function createDelayController(nominalMs = 50): DelayController;
```

Jitter = deviation `|Δarrival − Δtick × 50|` pushed into the ring (tick-number-aware so a coalesced TCP burst after a stall counts once, not as N violations); target = `clamp(nominal + INTERPOLATION_JITTER_MULT × p90, MIN, MAX)`; `effectiveDelayMs` slews toward target at `DELAY_SLEW_MS_PER_SEC`.

**Wiring — `NetworkSession`:** `case "tick"` calls `delayCtl.onTickArrival(performance.now(), message.n)`; `afterFrame` replaces the constant: `renderTime = serverTime - delayCtl.effectiveDelayMs(nowMs)`. `netStats()` now reports real `jitterMs`/`interpDelayMs`. Note `BUFFER_SIZE = 32` (1.6 s) comfortably covers a 450 ms delay — no change.

**Edge cases:** duplicate/out-of-order tick `n` (ignore non-increasing); reconnect resets the window (stale arrival gaps would read as one giant jitter spike — clear on `worldSync`); background tab (rAF stops but socket delivers — arrivals bunch; the p90 + slew absorb it, and the accumulator already drops debt).

**Tests:**
- `clock.test.ts`: (a) asymmetric jitter — feed pongs where RTT inflation is all on the return leg (offset error correlates with RTT); pure EWMA drifts by ~½ the mean inflation, min-RTT window stays within a few ms of truth — assert the new estimator beats the tolerance the old test allowed (±300 ms → ±25 ms); (b) slew: a 100 ms target-offset step moves `estimatedServerTimeMs` by ≤40 ms per simulated second; (c) >250 ms error snaps; (d) `rttMs()` still EWMA (existing expectations from `protocol.test.ts:99-113` carry over).
- `interpolation.test.ts`: controller converges — steady 50 ms arrivals → effective → 125 (floor); ±150 ms jittery arrivals → effective rises toward ~350–450, never exceeds MAX; slew rate bounded per step; jitter window forgets after 64 clean samples.
- `NetworkSession.test.ts`: ticks fed with jittered fake arrival timestamps → remote player sampling no longer clamps to newest (no freeze) — assert interpolated position lags latest sample.

**Docs:** `docs/tuning.md` (replace the `INTERPOLATION_DELAY_MS` bullet: MIN/MAX/JITTER_MULT/SLEW, `PING_INTERVAL_MS`, clock window constants), `docs/architecture.md` (multiplayer client paragraph: adaptive delay + min-RTT clock), `docs/testing.md` (add `lib/net/clock.test.ts` to the coverage row), `CHANGELOG.md`.

**Commit:** `net: adaptive interpolation delay and min-RTT clock sync`

---

## Slice 4 — Server event attribution (prerequisite for echo dedup)

Small, independently shippable, must deploy with-or-before the prediction slices.

**Files:** `lib/game/engine/state.ts`, `lib/game/engine/GameEngine.ts`, `server/room.test.ts`, docs.

```ts
// state.ts
export type AttributedGameEvent = GameEvent & { playerId?: PlayerId };
```

`GameEngine`: `private events: AttributedGameEvent[]`; `consumeEvents(): AttributedGameEvent[]` (assignable everywhere `GameEvent[]` is consumed); `emit` becomes:

```ts
private emit = (event: GameEvent, actorId?: PlayerId): void => {
  const attributed: AttributedGameEvent =
    this.authority === "server"
      ? { ...event, playerId: (event as AttributedGameEvent).playerId ?? actorId ?? this.actingPlayer }
      : event;
  this.events.push(attributed);
  ...
```

`actingPlayer` is already scoped around `stepPlayer` and `dispatch` (`GameEngine.ts:577-582, 698-704`), so `blockPlaced`/`blockBroken`/`attackSwung`/`jumped`/`landed` pick up the right actor; shared-system events (mob AI, TNT) stay unattributed — correct. Existing explicit `playerId`s (advancementUnlocked, join/leave) win via the `??` chain. `Room.tick` needs no change — `ev: events` already matches `TickMessage`'s type. Single-player path (`authority: "local"`) is untouched.

**Tests:** `room.test.ts` — extend "block edits propagate" (line 84): the receiving client's tick `ev` entry for `blockPlaced` carries `playerId` = the placer's id; an `attack` cmd's `attackSwung` likewise. `GameEngine.multiplayer.test.ts`: server engine stamps, local engine doesn't.

**Docs:** `docs/protocol.md` (Steady state: "events are attributed with the acting `playerId` where one exists"), `CHANGELOG.md`.

**Commit:** `server: attribute tick events to the acting player`

---

## Slice 5 — Prediction ledger + optimistic block placement (headline, part 1)

**Files:** new `lib/net/prediction.ts` + `lib/net/prediction.test.ts`, `lib/game/engine/blockChanges.ts`, `lib/game/engine/GameEngine.ts`, `lib/net/NetworkSession.ts`, `lib/net/NetworkSession.test.ts`, docs.

### 5a. Prev-aware journal — `blockChanges.ts`

Change `editJournal` to `Map<number, { block: number; prev: number }>` (prev captured on first write per drain window). Keep `drainEdits(): Array<[number, number]>` byte-identical for the server; add:

```ts
/** Every write since the last drain, with the pre-window value (prediction ledger + reverts). */
drainEditsDetailed(): Array<{ idx: number; block: number; prev: number }>;
```

Both drains clear the same journal.

### 5b. The ledger — `lib/net/prediction.ts` (pure, DOM-free, injected clock)

```ts
export type PredictedEdit = { idx: number; block: number; prev: number; confirmed: boolean };
export type Prediction = {
  id: number;
  kind: "place" | "break";
  edits: PredictedEdit[];                       // door = 2 cells, kelp stalk = n
  refund: { itemId: string; count: number } | null; // place only; null in creative
  deadlineMs: number;
};

export const PREDICTION_TIMEOUT_MIN_MS = 1000;
export const PREDICTION_TIMEOUT_MAX_MS = 5000;
export const predictionTimeoutMs = (rttMs: number): number =>
  Math.min(PREDICTION_TIMEOUT_MAX_MS, Math.max(PREDICTION_TIMEOUT_MIN_MS, 2 * rttMs + 200));
export const ECHO_SUPPRESS_EXTRA_MS = 3000;     // suppress window outlives the entry

export type JournalResolution = { refunds: Array<{ itemId: string; count: number }> };
export type PredictionLedger = {
  add(kind: "place" | "break", edits: Array<{ idx: number; block: number; prev: number }>,
      refund: Prediction["refund"], nowMs: number, rttMs: number): void;
  /** One authoritative journal write: resolves EVERY prediction touching idx.
   *  Matching value ⇒ confirm that edit; when all of a prediction's edits confirm it retires.
   *  Mismatch ⇒ drop the prediction, collect its refund. Never mutates the world. */
  onJournal(idx: number, block: number): JournalResolution;
  /** Expired predictions to revert (unconfirmed edits only, newest-first). */
  expire(nowMs: number): Prediction[];
  /** Echo dedup: own blockPlaced/blockBroken at idx within the suppress window? */
  shouldSuppress(idx: number, nowMs: number): boolean;
  clear(): void;
  size(): number;
};
export function createPredictionLedger(): PredictionLedger;
```

Reconciliation policy (deliberately simple, server inventory delta is the ultimate safety net): **any journal write to an idx resolves all predictions on that idx** — the value either confirms one edit or drops the whole prediction (with refund for places). This also guarantees a revert never uses a `prev` that a server write has since invalidated. `shouldSuppress` uses a separate `Map<idx, untilMs>` populated on `add` with `deadlineMs + ECHO_SUPPRESS_EXTRA_MS`, expiring in `expire()` — it must outlive confirm/retire because the event echo rides the *same* tick that confirms (applyBlocks runs before the `ev` loop in `processServerFrame`).

### 5c. Predicted placement — `GameEngine.predictPlaceBlock()`

The replica dispatches through the full engine? No — `placeBlock` is the overloaded right-click (spear/tame/feed/sit/trade/board/interact/summon/fish/vehicle/useItem before placement, `GameEngine.ts:879-906`), and most of those must stay server-owned on a replica. **Chosen approach: engine-side gated predict method** (the replica engine has the world/raycast/inventory logic; NetworkSession stays orchestration):

```ts
/** Replica-only. Locally applies a right-click iff it would fall through to pure
 *  block placement; conservative gates bail to null (server handles it, status quo). */
predictPlaceBlock(): {
  edits: Array<{ idx: number; block: number; prev: number }>;
  refund: { itemId: string; count: number } | null;
} | null
```

Internals: guard `this.replica`; gates — held slot is `kind === "block"`, `count > 0`, not Bedrock (excludes every item-driven branch: spear/boat/hoe/seeds/bone-meal/food/rod/totem); no mob or vehicle near the aim ray within `MINE_REACH` (reuse/extract the aim-pick used by `tryFeedAimedMob`/`tryAttackMob`; conservative distance check for vehicles is fine — a false "don't predict" is just status quo); raycast hit block has no `INTERACTIVE_BLOCKS` entry (exported at `interact.ts:33` — covers doors/beds/stations/chests); player alive, inventory closed, `canEditBlocks`. Then: discard-drain `state.blockChanges.drainEditsDetailed()` (the replica journal is never drained today, so first use must flush historical noise), snapshot `player.inventory` ref, call `placeSelectedBlock(state, player, this.emit)` (validates air/water, self-entombment, consumes stack, **emits `blockPlaced` → instant local sound/particles via the shell's normal drain**), drain detailed → `edits`; `refund = inventoryRefChanged ? { itemId: heldSlot.id, count: 1 } : null` (creative/no-op ⇒ null). Empty `edits` ⇒ return null (placement refused locally — don't even send? No: **still send the cmd** — server precedence may do something the gate missed; prediction and sending are independent).

### 5d. Wiring — `NetworkSession`

`routeDispatch` for `placeBlock`:

```ts
if (command.type === "placeBlock" && ws?.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN && status === "online") {
  const predicted = engine.predictPlaceBlock();
  if (predicted) ledger.add("place", predicted.edits, predicted.refund, performance.now(), clock.rttMs());
}
sendCmd(command);   // always — server remains authoritative
```

`applyBlocks` becomes ledger-aware:

```ts
const applyBlocks = (blocks: Array<[number, number]>) => {
  for (const [idx, block] of blocks) {
    const { refunds } = ledger.onJournal(idx, block);
    for (const r of refunds) refundToInventory(r);          // adjustSlotCount(+count, selectedSlot pref)
    if (worldValueAt(idx) !== block) state.blockChanges.set(x, y, z, block);  // skip redundant remesh on confirms
  }
  state.blockChanges.drainEditsDetailed();                   // server writes never register as predictions
  if (blocks.length > 0) state.worldMeshDirty = true;
};
```

Ordering note: `applyBlocks` runs before `applySelfDelta` in the tick case (`NetworkSession.ts:424-426`) — so an override-refund applied here is immediately overwritten by an authoritative `inventorySlots` delta in the same tick when one exists. Correct final state either way.

Event suppression in the tick `ev` loop (before `pendingEvents.push` **and** `callbacks.onEvent`):

```ts
if ((ev.type === "blockPlaced" || ev.type === "blockBroken")
    && ev.playerId === playerId
    && ledger.shouldSuppress(state.world.index(ev.x, ev.y, ev.z), now)) continue;
```

Timeout sweep in `afterFrame`:

```ts
for (const p of ledger.expire(nowMs)) {
  for (const e of [...p.edits].reverse()) if (!e.confirmed) state.blockChanges.set(x(e), y(e), z(e), e.prev); // relights
  if (p.refund) refundToInventory(p.refund);
  state.blockChanges.drainEditsDetailed();  // reverts aren't predictions either
}
```

`worldSync` (`applyWorldSync`) and reconnect: `ledger.clear()` — the sync is a full keyframe and `resync` re-arms the full self-delta server-side.

**Edge cases (explicit):**
- **Rejected place** (raced another player / server precedence diverged): no journal entry, no SelfDelta ⇒ timeout revert restores block + lighting + refunds the item within `max(2×RTT+200, 1000)` ms. Bounded, visible, honest.
- **Lost race, different block at same voxel**: journal mismatch ⇒ immediate override (server block appears), refund now; if the place actually succeeded then got broken same-tick (journal coalesces last-write-wins per tick, `blockChanges.ts:27`), the spurious refund is corrected by the next `inventorySlots` delta.
- **Late confirm after timeout revert**: journal re-applies server truth (block flickers back); suppress window (deadline + 3000 ms) still swallows the echo — no double sound; state converges.
- **Concurrent inventory-changing delta while a place is pending**: `inventorySlots` overwrite may transiently un-decrement the hotbar count until the place's own delta lands. Known cosmetic limitation — document, don't compensate.
- **Refund into a full inventory** (player filled the slot meanwhile): `adjustSlotCount` returns null ⇒ drop the refund silently (rare double-failure; next full delta reconciles). 
- **Predictions while reconnecting/offline**: gated on socket OPEN + status online — `sendCmd` silently drops when closed today, and a phantom un-sent edit must never be predicted.
- **Door = 2 edits**: retires only when both cells confirm; a mismatch on either drops and reverts the unconfirmed cell(s) only.

**`selectSlot` stays as-is** (already optimistic, server confirms via delta).

**Tests:**
- `prediction.test.ts` (pure): confirm exact-match; multi-edit (door) partial-confirm then retire; override collects refund and drops siblings; expire returns newest-first unconfirmed edits and honors `max(2×RTT+200, 1000..5000)`; suppress window spans add→deadline+3000 and survives confirm; clear().
- `blockChanges.test`-style additions (colocate in an existing engine test or new file): `drainEditsDetailed` captures prev-of-window, coalesces same-idx writes keeping first prev; `drainEdits` unchanged shape.
- `NetworkSession.test.ts` (FakeSocket): place → block in replica world instantly + refund captured; tick with matching journal → no revert, own attributed `blockPlaced` **not** in `drainEvents()`, other-player events pass through; tick with mismatching journal → server block + inventory refunded; no tick → fake-clock advance past deadline → block and lighting-path write reverted (assert `world.get` back to prev), item refunded; worldSync clears pending predictions.
- Existing test to update: "routes gameplay commands to the wire but keeps presentation on the replica" — placeBlock now also mutates the replica when the gate passes; adjust or point its assertion at a non-predicted command (e.g. `craft`).

**Docs:** `docs/architecture.md` (multiplayer client: prediction ledger paragraph — replica predicts placement, journal confirms/overrides, timeout reverts), `docs/protocol.md` (Trust model: unchanged wire; client-side prediction note), `docs/tuning.md` (PREDICTION_TIMEOUT_MIN/MAX, formula, ECHO_SUPPRESS_EXTRA_MS), `docs/testing.md` (coverage row: `lib/net/prediction.test.ts`), `CHANGELOG.md` (Added).

**Commit:** `net: optimistic block placement with a prediction ledger`

---

## Slice 6 — Predictive block breaking + cosmetic swing (headline, part 2)

**Files:** `lib/game/engine/systems/mining.ts`, `lib/game/engine/GameEngine.ts`, `lib/net/NetworkSession.ts`, `lib/game/engine/systems/mining.test.ts`, `lib/net/NetworkSession.test.ts`, docs.

**`tickMining` mode — replace the boolean:**

```ts
opts: { authority?: "full" | "cosmetic" | "predict" } = {}   // default "full"
```

- `"cosmetic"` keeps today's cap (retained for tests and as the fallback for non-predictable targets).
