## Slice 3 — Clock hardening + adaptive interpolation delay

**Files:** `lib/net/clock.ts`, `lib/net/interpolation.ts`, `lib/net/NetworkSession.ts`, new `lib/net/clock.test.ts` (move the "clock sync" describe out of `protocol.test.ts`), `lib/net/interpolation.test.ts`, docs.

**Clock hardening — `clock.ts`.** Keep the `ClockSync` surface; change internals to NTP-style best-sample offset with slewed application:

```ts
export function createClockSync(alpha = 0.2): ClockSync;
// internals:
//  - rtt: EWMA α=0.2 (unchanged — display + prediction timeouts)
//  - window: ring of last OFFSET_WINDOW_SIZE {rtt, offset} pong samples
//  - targetOffset = offset of the min-RTT sample in the window
//    (least queue-delay asymmetry ⇒ most trustworthy half-RTT split)
//  - appliedOffset slews toward targetOffset at OFFSET_SLEW_MS_PER_SEC inside
//    estimatedServerTimeMs (using nowMs deltas), except |error| > OFFSET_SNAP_MS → snap
//    (first sample and post-reconnect discontinuities snap too)
```

Constants: `OFFSET_WINDOW_SIZE = 16`, `OFFSET_SLEW_MS_PER_SEC = 40`, `OFFSET_SNAP_MS = 250`.

**Ping cadence:** extract `PING_INTERVAL_MS = 1000` in `NetworkSession.ts` (was inline `2000` at line 623). At 1 Hz the 16-sample window spans 16 s; a ping frame is ~40 bytes — negligible.

**Adaptive delay — `interpolation.ts`** (stays pure math, DOM-free):

```ts
export const INTERPOLATION_DELAY_MIN_MS = 125;   // old fixed value becomes the floor
export const INTERPOLATION_DELAY_MAX_MS = 450;
export const INTERPOLATION_JITTER_MULT = 2;
export const DELAY_SLEW_MS_PER_SEC = 60;         // ≤6% render-time warp, imperceptible
const JITTER_WINDOW = 64;                        // ~3.2 s of tick arrivals

export type DelayController = {
  /** Feed one tick frame's arrival (client wall clock + server tick number). */
  onTickArrival(nowMs: number, tickN: number): void;
  /** Slewed effective delay for this frame (call once per rAF). */
  effectiveDelayMs(nowMs: number): number;
  /** p90 |inter-arrival − nominal| over the window (for netStats). */
  jitterMs(): number;
};
export function createDelayController(nominalMs = 50): DelayController;
```

Jitter = deviation `|Δarrival − Δtick × 50|` pushed into the ring (tick-number-aware so a coalesced TCP burst after a stall counts once, not as N violations); target = `clamp(nominal + INTERPOLATION_JITTER_MULT × p90, MIN, MAX)`; `effectiveDelayMs` slews toward target at `DELAY_SLEW_MS_PER_SEC`.

**Wiring — `NetworkSession`:** `case "tick"` calls `delayCtl.onTickArrival(performance.now(), message.n)`; `afterFrame` replaces the constant: `renderTime = serverTime - delayCtl.effectiveDelayMs(nowMs)`. `netStats()` now reports real `jitterMs`/`interpDelayMs`. Note `BUFFER_SIZE = 32` (1.6 s) comfortably covers a 450 ms delay — no change.

**Edge cases:** duplicate/out-of-order tick `n` (ignore non-increasing); reconnect resets the window (stale arrival gaps would read as one giant jitter spike — clear on `worldSync`); background tab (rAF stops but socket delivers — arrivals bunch; the p90 + slew absorb it, and the accumulator already drops debt).

**Tests:**
- `clock.test.ts`: (a) asymmetric jitter — feed pongs where RTT inflation is all on the return leg (offset error correlates with RTT); pure EWMA drifts by ~½ the mean inflation, min-RTT window stays within a few ms of truth — assert the new estimator beats the tolerance the old test allowed (±300 ms → ±25 ms); (b) slew: a 100 ms target-offset step moves `estimatedServerTimeMs` by ≤40 ms per simulated second; (c) >250 ms error snaps; (d) `rttMs()` still EWMA (existing expectations from `protocol.test.ts:99-113` carry over).
- `interpolation.test.ts`: controller converges — steady 50 ms arrivals → effective → 125 (floor); ±150 ms jittery arrivals → effective rises toward ~350–450, never exceeds MAX; slew rate bounded per step; jitter window forgets after 64 clean samples.
- `NetworkSession.test.ts`: ticks fed with jittered fake arrival timestamps → remote player sampling no longer clamps to newest (no freeze) — assert interpolated position lags latest sample.

